\subsection{Adaptive Inference-Time Compute}
\label{sec:adaptive_k_method}

Fixed $K{=}10$ sampling is wasteful: most steps are ``easy'' and do not benefit from correction, while $K{=}1$ correction fails because the CRM receives an uncentered input (Section~\ref{sec:failure_modes}). We propose an adaptive mechanism based on a cheap uncertainty probe.

\paragraph{$K{=}2$ $L_2$ probe.}
We draw $K{=}2$ samples from $\pi_\theta$ in a single forward pass (${\sim}$255\,ms with batched inference, comparable to $K{=}1$; Appendix~\ref{sec:latency}) and compute their pairwise $L_2$ distance $d_2 = \|a^{(1)} - a^{(2)}\|_2$. This distance correlates near-perfectly with the full $K{=}10$ sample variance ($\rho{=}0.9979$; Section~\ref{sec:adaptive_k}), providing a per-step uncertainty estimate at minimal cost.

\paragraph{Decision rule.}
At each control step:
\begin{itemize}[nosep]
  \item If $d_2 \leq \tau$: the step is ``easy''; use the $K{=}2$ sample mean directly without CRM correction.
  \item If $d_2 > \tau$: the step is ``hard''; extend to $K{=}10$ samples, apply argmin selection and CRM correction.
\end{itemize}
The threshold $\tau$ is calibrated from the empirical distribution of $d_2$ values in the CRM training data, using percentile cutoffs (P25, P50, P75, P90) to trade off between average compute and task success rate (Section~\ref{sec:adaptive_k}).

CRM inference adds ${\sim}$0.3\,ms; the VLA forward pass (${\sim}$255\,ms) dominates latency. We evaluate three alternative uncertainty signals: flow-matching velocity divergence (FMVD, $\rho{=}0.08$), CRM correction magnitude at $K{=}1$ ($\rho{=}0.15$), and CRM correction magnitude at $K{=}2$ ($\rho{=}0.13$). All prove uncorrelated with action-space variance; the $K{=}2$ $L_2$ distance is the only viable signal (Section~\ref{sec:adaptive_k}).

