\subsection{Adaptive Inference-Time Compute}
\label{sec:adaptive_k_method}

Two findings from our component analysis motivate an adaptive mechanism: (i)~learned correction is the dominant contributor to task improvement (Section~\ref{sec:main_results}), but (ii)~correction on uncentered inputs ($K{=}1$) degrades below baseline (Section~\ref{sec:failure_modes}). Fixed $K{=}10$ sampling is wasteful---most steps are easy---yet $K{=}1$ correction fails. The solution is a per-step uncertainty probe that decides \emph{when} to invest additional compute.

\paragraph{Why $K{=}2$.}
$K{=}2$ is the minimum sample size that admits a pairwise disagreement measure: one sample carries no spread information, while two samples yield a single $L_2$ distance that serves as a non-trivial uncertainty proxy. This minimal probe is also computationally free: under batched inference with shared prefix encoding, $K{=}2$ latency (${\sim}$255\,ms) matches $K{=}1$ (Appendix~\ref{sec:latency}), because the flow-matching denoising of both samples runs in parallel on the same GPU. Empirically, the $K{=}2$ $L_2$ distance correlates near-perfectly with the full $K{=}10$ sample variance ($\rho{=}0.9979$, Spearman; Section~\ref{sec:adaptive_k}), so increasing to $K{=}3$ or beyond for the probe would add latency with negligible signal gain. Three alternative uncertainty signals---flow-matching velocity divergence (FMVD, $\rho{=}0.08$), CRM correction magnitude at $K{=}1$ ($\rho{=}0.15$) and $K{=}2$ ($\rho{=}0.13$)---all fail (Section~\ref{sec:adaptive_k}), confirming $K{=}2$ $L_2$ as the only viable per-step signal.

\paragraph{Three-step adaptive pipeline.}
At each control step, the pipeline executes:
\begin{enumerate}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{Uncertainty probe.} Draw $K{=}2$ samples from $\pi_\theta$ in a single batched forward pass and compute their pairwise $L_2$ distance $d_2 = \|a^{(1)} - a^{(2)}\|_2$.
  \item \textbf{Adaptive decision.} If $d_2 \leq \tau$ (low uncertainty), use the $K{=}2$ sample mean directly---no further computation. If $d_2 > \tau$ (high uncertainty), extend to $K{=}10$ samples and apply argmin selection~(\ref{eq:argmin}).
  \item \textbf{Learned correction.} For high-uncertainty steps only, the CRM predicts a bounded residual $\boldsymbol{\delta}$ ($\|\boldsymbol{\delta}\|_2 \leq \varepsilon$) on the selected action~(\ref{eq:crm}).
\end{enumerate}
The threshold $\tau$ is a percentile of the empirical $d_2$ distribution from CRM training data. Higher $\tau$ (e.g., P90) triggers correction on fewer steps, reducing compute; lower $\tau$ (e.g., P25) triggers more aggressively. Section~\ref{sec:adaptive_k} evaluates this trade-off and shows that the optimal threshold is task-difficulty-dependent: P50 on harder tasks (Object), P90 on easier tasks (Spatial). CRM inference adds ${\sim}$0.3\,ms; the VLA forward pass (${\sim}$255\,ms) dominates latency.

